            "<strong>Purpose.</strong> The doctor performs the puncture; the nurse makes it safe, tolerable and useful. Before any bedside procedure the nurse confirms the <strong>indication and the written consent</strong> (with the patient's own understanding checked), the <strong>contraindications</strong> relevant to that procedure, the <strong>coagulation state</strong> where bleeding matters (anticoagulants held as instructed, platelet count and clotting results seen), <strong>allergies</strong> to chlorhexidine, iodine, latex and local anaesthetic, the <strong>baseline observations</strong>, the <strong>imaging</strong> where the site depends on it, and that the <strong>correct side and site are agreed aloud</strong> in a brief time-out with the operator; prepares the <strong>trolley</strong> (sterile pack, antiseptic, local anaesthetic with the dose checked against the patient's weight, the procedure-specific needles and containers, specimen bottles and request forms pre-labelled with the patient's identifiers and the order of collection, dressings, sharps bin), the <strong>environment</strong> (privacy, light, bed at working height, the bed brakes on, oxygen and suction within reach) and the <strong>patient</strong> (bladder emptied where relevant, analgesia or anxiolysis given in time to work, the position explained and rehearsed, a second nurse or parent to hold a child); during the procedure <strong>holds the position</strong>, <strong>watches the patient rather than the needle</strong> — colour, breathing, pulse, saturation, pain, faintness — talks the patient through each step, hands sterile items to the operator without contaminating the field, and <strong>receives, caps, labels and numbers specimens in the order collected</strong>; afterwards applies the dressing, positions the patient, records the time, operator, site, amount and appearance of fluid removed, specimens sent, the patient's tolerance and the observations, sends specimens by hand immediately when the test is time-sensitive, and begins the <strong>procedure-specific aftercare and observations</strong>. The count of needles and sharps used at the bedside is as real as in theatre: every needle that came out of a packet goes into the sharps bin, and the operator does not leave until it has.",
