            "<strong>Eye irrigation</strong> washes a chemical, dust, an insect or a loose foreign body from the eye and is <strong>started within seconds for a chemical splash</strong> — caustic soda, cement, bleach, battery acid, pepper spray, a cleaning agent — before any history is taken, because the damage is done in minutes and <strong>alkalis go on burning for as long as they remain</strong>. The patient sits or lies with the head tilted towards the affected side so that the fluid does not run into the other eye, a towel and a receiver against the cheek; the nurse washes her hands, puts on gloves, instils a drop of local anaesthetic if prescribed and available (the eye is too painful to open otherwise), <strong>everts the lids and sweeps the fornices with a moist cotton bud</strong> to remove particles of lime or cement that keep releasing alkali, holds the lids open with a thumb and finger on the bony margins (never pressing on the globe), and <strong>irrigates with 0.9 per cent saline, Ringer's lactate or clean water at room temperature from a giving set, a bag, a bottle or a tap</strong>, directed at the inner canthus and across the eye to the outer side from about 2–3 cm, asking the patient to look up, down and to each side, <strong>for at least fifteen to twenty minutes and a minimum of a litre for a chemical injury, continuing until the pH of the conjunctival sac tested with litmus or urine-dipstick paper is 7–7.5 and stays so five minutes after stopping</strong>; visual acuity is recorded afterwards, the substance identified, and every chemical injury, penetrating injury or persistent pain, photophobia or reduced vision is referred to the eye unit the same day. A <strong>penetrating injury or a foreign body embedded in the cornea is not irrigated or touched</strong>: the eye is covered with a rigid shield (or the bottom of a paper cup) without pressure, the other eye covered to limit movement, and the patient kept nil by mouth and sent urgently. <strong>Ear irrigation</strong> (syringing) removes impacted wax or a non-vegetable foreign body <strong>only after otoscopy has confirmed an intact drum</strong> and <strong>never where there is a perforation, a grommet, previous ear surgery, an only hearing ear, otitis externa, a seed or bean (which swells), a live insect (killed first with oil) or a button battery</strong>: wax is softened with olive oil or sodium bicarbonate drops for three to five days first; the patient sits with the head tilted slightly towards the affected side and a receiver held below the ear; the nurse straightens the canal by pulling the <strong>pinna up and back (down and back in a young child)</strong>, and with an electronic irrigator or a large syringe with a soft tip directs <strong>water at body temperature (37 °C — cold or hot water causes violent vertigo and vomiting) along the roof and posterior wall of the canal, never straight at the drum</strong>, in gentle pulses, inspecting between pulses, and stops at once for pain, dizziness, bleeding or a sudden change in hearing; the canal is dried with a wick of cotton wool, the drum re-examined, the result and the appearance recorded, and the patient told that the ear may feel odd for an hour; failure after two attempts, pain, bleeding or a foreign body that will not come is referred to the ear, nose and throat clinic, not forced. <strong>Assisting with anterior nasal packing</strong> for an epistaxis that first aid has not stopped: the patient sits upright leaning forward with a receiver and tissues, the nurse records the pulse and pressure (and sets up intravenous access and sends blood for a group and haemoglobin in heavy or anticoagulated bleeding), lays out the headlight, speculum, suction, a topical anaesthetic–vasoconstrictor spray or soaked cotton wool, silver nitrate sticks for a visible bleeding point, and the nasal tampon or ribbon gauze impregnated with paraffin or antiseptic, supports the patient's head during insertion, and afterwards tapes the tampon's string to the cheek, applies a nasal bolster, checks the back of the throat for continued bleeding, gives analgesia (not aspirin or non-steroidals), teaches the patient to avoid nose-blowing, hot drinks, bending and straining, and keeps the pack in for the <strong>24–48 hours</strong> the clinician specifies with the prophylactic antibiotic prescribed for packs left longer; a posterior pack or balloon catheter needs admission, saturation monitoring and hourly observations because it can obstruct the airway, and bleeding that continues around a pack is reported at once.",
