            "<strong>Types.</strong> A fracture is described by its pattern — <strong>transverse</strong> (straight across), <strong>oblique</strong> (angled), <strong>spiral</strong> (twisting), <strong>comminuted</strong> (many fragments), <strong>impacted</strong> (ends driven together), <strong>greenstick</strong> (a child's bone bends and cracks on one side), <strong>avulsion</strong> (a fragment pulled off by a tendon), <strong>pathological</strong> (through diseased bone — metastasis, osteomyelitis, osteoporosis) and <strong>stress</strong> — by displacement and angulation, by whether it involves a joint or a growth plate, and above all by whether it is <strong>closed or open</strong>. <strong>An open fracture is an emergency</strong> because the bone is contaminated from the moment the skin breaks: the nurse <strong>controls bleeding with pressure around the wound</strong>, <strong>removes gross contamination</strong> (grass, cloth) without probing, <strong>photographs the wound if possible</strong> so that it need not be uncovered repeatedly, <strong>covers it with a sterile saline-soaked dressing</strong> and an occlusive layer, <strong>does not push protruding bone back</strong>, <strong>splints the limb</strong> in the position found after a neurovascular check, gives analgesia, <strong>starts the prescribed intravenous antibiotic within an hour</strong> of arrival, confirms <strong>tetanus</strong> cover, keeps the patient <strong>nil by mouth</strong>, and arranges urgent theatre for debridement and stabilisation; the time of injury, the time of antibiotic and every neurovascular check are written down. A <strong>closed fracture</strong> is immobilised, elevated, iced through a cloth for twenty minutes at a time, given analgesia, and assessed for the neurovascular status recorded in the cast lesson; <strong>femoral and pelvic fractures</strong> bleed litres into the tissues, so the pulse and pressure are monitored, a pelvic binder applied at the level of the greater trochanters for a suspected unstable pelvis, and blood crossmatched. <strong>Fat embolism</strong> — breathlessness, confusion or restlessness and a <strong>petechial rash over the chest, axillae and conjunctivae</strong> 24–72 hours after a long-bone or pelvic fracture — is reported at once and managed with oxygen and support; its prevention is early immobilisation and fixation. The other complications — infection, delayed union and non-union, malunion, joint stiffness, avascular necrosis (the scaphoid and the femoral neck), nerve and vessel injury, complex regional pain, and <strong>compartment syndrome</strong>, in which pain out of proportion and on passive stretch is the first sign and a pulse the last to go — are watched for by the nurse who sees the limb every hour.",
