          alt: "Fractures — types, and the complication that costs a limb. Transverse, straight across; oblique, angled; comminuted, many fragments; greenstick, a child's bone bends; impacted, driven together; open, through the skin. Open equals urgent: the skin is broken so the bone is contaminated — cover with sterile dressing, antibiotics, tetanus cover, theatre. Compartment syndrome, the six Ps and which comes first: pain out of all proportion, worse on passive stretch — first; paraesthesia, pins and needles then numbness; pallor, pale cool skin; paralysis, cannot move it, already late; pulseless, very late, the limb may already be lost; perishing cold, the final sign. Waiting for a pulse to disappear is waiting too long: pressure inside the compartment closes the small vessels feeding muscle and nerve long before it can close a major artery; a present pulse gives false reassurance; muscle dies within 4 to 6 hours and the damage is permanent. Act on pain: elevate no higher than the heart, split the cast and all padding down to skin, report immediately; definitive treatment is surgical fasciotomy. Other fracture complications: fat embolism, infection, non-union, DVT.",
