        "Lumbar puncture is deferred for signs of raised intracranial pressure (antibiotics given and a scan obtained first), coagulopathy, local infection and cardiorespiratory compromise; the patient curls laterally with the chin tucked (an infant's neck never flexed), the needle enters at L3–4 or L4–5 below the cord, the antiseptic dries first, the fluid drips into numbered bottles with a paired blood glucose and goes to the laboratory at once; aftercare is comfort, fluids, neurological observations and site checks, without enforced bed rest.",
        "Paracentesis needs an empty bladder, a left-lower-quadrant site, fifteen-minute observations, a drain removed within six hours and albumin when more than five litres are removed; pleural aspiration is done sitting forward, above the rib, stopped for cough or chest pain, and limited to 1–1.5 litres; bone-marrow aspiration from the posterior iliac crest is followed by firm pressure, a pressure dressing, lying on the site and hourly checks for bleeding; a chemical eye splash is irrigated within seconds for at least fifteen to twenty minutes until the conjunctival pH is 7–7.5 (a penetrating injury is shielded, never irrigated), an ear is irrigated only with an intact drum using water at 37 °C along the canal wall, and a nasal pack stays 24–48 hours with the airway watched.",
