            "<strong>Pain</strong> is assessed by asking while the patient can answer and by watching the face, the limbs and the breathing when she cannot (a validated behavioural scale); analgesia is given <strong>regularly by the clock, by mouth while swallowing is safe, by the ladder</strong> (paracetamol and a non-steroidal, then a weak opioid, then <strong>oral morphine</strong>, which is available in Ghana as a solution and tablets and is started low — 2.5–5 mg four-hourly in the opioid-naive, half in the frail and in renal failure — and titrated by the previous day's total with a <strong>breakthrough dose of one-sixth of the daily dose</strong> available at any time), and converted to the <strong>subcutaneous route</strong> through an indwelling butterfly cannula in the chest wall, abdomen or upper arm (site changed when red or every few days, labelled, with the drugs, doses and times on a chart) as intermittent injections or a <strong>syringe driver over 24 hours</strong> when swallowing fails — the oral morphine dose halved for subcutaneous use — with laxatives and an antiemetic from the first opioid dose and the family taught that morphine given for pain at these doses <strong>does not hasten death</strong> and is not addiction. <strong>Breathlessness</strong> is eased by sitting up or on the side, a <strong>fan or open window on the face</strong>, calm presence, a small dose of opioid, and oxygen only if hypoxaemic and it helps; <strong>respiratory secretions</strong> by <strong>repositioning onto the side with the head slightly down</strong>, stopping intravenous fluids, gentle oropharyngeal suction only if it clearly helps (it usually does not and distresses), and <strong>hyoscine butylbromide or glycopyrronium subcutaneously</strong> early rather than late; <strong>nausea</strong> by the antiemetic matched to the cause; <strong>agitation and delirium</strong> by looking first for a full bladder, faecal loading, pain, a wet bed, fear, or a drug, then a quiet voice, a familiar face, low light, and a benzodiazepine or haloperidol as prescribed; <strong>fever</strong> by paracetamol and sponging for comfort. <strong>Mouth care every one to two hours</strong> — moist swabs, water sprays or ice chips, lip balm, dentures cleaned or removed, candida treated — is the single procedure that most relieves the dying and most involves the family; <strong>eyes</strong> are kept moist and clean; <strong>skin</strong> is kept clean, dry and free of pressure by <strong>gentle repositioning for comfort</strong> every few hours on a pressure-relieving mattress, not by a two-hourly regimen that wakes and hurts; a <strong>catheter</strong> is placed if retention or wet skin is distressing, or absorbent pads used with frequent changes; the <strong>bowel</strong> is checked for loading and relieved; the room is kept clean and odour-free; and <strong>food and drink are offered, never forced</strong> — sips, tastes and moistening, with the family told that stopping eating is part of dying and that artificial hydration rarely helps and often adds secretions and oedema. <strong>Nursing presence</strong> continues: the dying patient is spoken to by name, told what is being done, never discussed over, and never left alone at the end if the family cannot be there; the <strong>spiritual care</strong> the patient wants — a pastor, an imam, a traditional elder, prayers, a hymn, the Qur'an read, a rite performed — is arranged by the nurse, not left to chance; and children in the family are allowed to visit with preparation. In the Ghanaian setting the nurse also helps the family with the practical matters they will ask about — whether the patient can be taken home to die, what transport and medicines are needed for that, and what documents the family will need afterwards — because many families want the death at home and a good discharge for dying is a nursing procedure too.",
