            "The family is told of the death <strong>in person, in private, sitting down, by name</strong>, with the word ‘died’ used plainly and the time given, by the nurse or doctor who knew the patient, with silence allowed, with tissues, water and time, and with questions answered honestly — what happened in the last minutes, whether she suffered, whether they could have done anything (usually not, and said so). The nurse explains what happens next — viewing the body, the death certificate and registration, the mortuary and its fees, collecting the body, the property — writes the essentials down because nothing said in the first hour is remembered, identifies the one relative who will take the lead, calls someone to accompany a relative who is alone, and offers the chaplain, imam or counsellor. A relative who screams, faints, collapses or becomes aggressive is met with calm presence and safety, not sedation or security as the first response. Where the family must travel from a distance, the body is kept until they can view it, and a relative arriving at night is met, not sent away. Bereavement follow-up — a telephone call, a visit from the community health nurse, information about grief and about support groups — is arranged where it exists, and sudden deaths, children's deaths, deaths of mothers and deaths after errors need more of it. <strong>Staff</strong> are affected too: a short <strong>debrief</strong> after a distressing death or a resuscitation — what happened, what went well, what should change, how people are — is a professional practice and not a weakness, and the student nurse who saw her first death is asked how she is before the next admission arrives.",
